Give the evaluator a readable statistics report, and count calls safely.

nanopynix embeds the evaluator, and it needs three things that upstream Nix
does not give an embedded caller.

**A report that a caller can read.** `EvalState::printStatistics` builds the
report and then writes it, to stderr or to the file that `NIX_SHOW_STATS_PATH`
names. A library caller can use neither. This patch splits the function: a new
`statisticsJSON` builds the report and returns it, and `printStatistics` keeps
its behavior and calls the new function. No output changes.

**A setting, rather than an environment variable.** `countCalls` came from
`getEnv("NIX_COUNT_CALLS")` at the moment the evaluator was constructed, so
the choice belonged to the whole process. nanopynix gives each evaluator its
own settings, and it builds an evaluator while other evaluators run, so
`setenv` from one thread is a data race. `count-calls` is now an ordinary
`EvalSettings` option. The environment variable still turns counting on, so a
program that already uses it keeps working.

**Counters that two evaluators can share.** `primOpCalls`, `functionCalls` and
`attrSelects` were `boost::unordered_flat_map`, and `nix` the command writes
them from one thread only. nanopynix gives each evaluator its own thread, so
two threads write the same map. They are `boost::concurrent_flat_map` here,
behind a `ref`, and each write is a `try_emplace_or_visit`. The read in
`statisticsJSON` uses `visit_all`.

One upstream defect goes with them. `printStatistics` wrote
`auto list = topObj["attributes"]`, which copies the value out of the
document, so every attribute count was built and then discarded. The `&` is
here.

This file applies to 2.34. `nix-2.35-count-calls.patch` is the same change for
2.35 and for git, where only the line numbers differ. 2.31 gets neither: see
the `nixPatches` comment in `default.nix`.

